// A salt diapir. Rock salt (mat 1) has risen from its mother layer at the
// bottom of the section, through four overlying strata (mats 2 to 5 upward),
// as a stem that narrows to a neck and spreads into an overhanging cap. As
// it rose it dragged the strata up against its flanks: each bedding plane,
// flat far from the salt, bends upward over its last half unit and meets the
// salt 16 to 24 degrees above horizontal. Stratum 4 (the third up) reaches in
// under both overhangs and fills the armpits between cap and stem; stratum 5
// drapes over the cap. Sections like this are what geomechanical models of
// salt basins (well planning, subsalt imaging) are built on.
//
// What this domain is for. A strongly non-convex material region. The salt
// is one surface -- a layer, a stem, a neck and a cap with two overhangs.
// Round it,
// six interior triple junctions are strung along one curved interface, where
// each bedding plane lands on the salt (salt, stratum k, stratum k+1). The
// salt's face turns continuously, so the six are ILL-POSED each at its own
// angle, and two of them sit at the necks, where the face is turning fastest.
// Between the cap's underside and the stem each armpit is a concave pocket of
// stratum 4 bounded by salt on three sides, at least 5 elements across.
//
// Strata 2, 3 and 4 are each one material on two surfaces, one either side
// of the diapir; stratum 5 is one surface over the top. Every bedding plane
// meets the walls at a right angle. At NP = 100 (h = 0.04 here) a stratum is
// about 11 elements deep and 9 remain over the crest of the cap.
//
// The salt's outline and the dragged bedding planes are generated (a
// centripetal Catmull-Rom curve through hand-placed control points, and
// y = y0 + (yJ - y0) exp(-|x - xJ| / 0.3) for the drag), then written as
// Splines through samples 0.05 apart, split exactly at the junctions.

// ---- generated geometry ----
